The tool library keeps a checkout sheet. Volunteers need a small
list of things still out, but the export also includes returned
items and a shelf column they don't need at the counter. Copying
only the useful cells by hand would work once. A better use of
the terminal is to describe which rows qualify and which columns
to show, leaving the original checkout sheet alone.

\begin{objectives}
\item Identify tab-separated rows and fields even when tabs look like spaces.
\item Extract or combine columns with \texttt{cut} and \texttt{paste}.
\item Use a small \texttt{awk} rule to select records by field and verify the result.
\end{objectives}
\IndexCommand{cut}
\IndexCommand{paste}
\IndexCommand{awk}
\IndexTerm{tab-separated values}

\section{Find the field boundary}
For this chapter, use a fresh temporary directory and a portable
\cmd|sh| session. Run the first two commands separately. If
either fails, stop; don't create these files anywhere else.

\begin{terminal}
practice=$(mktemp -d /tmp/utp21.XXXXXX)
cd "$practice"
printf '%s\t%s\t%s\n' \
  item shelf state \
  'Hand saw' A2 out \
  'Clamp set' B1 in \
  'Step ladder' '' out \
  'Out bin' C1 in > loans.tsv
\end{terminal}

The format contains three \cmd|%s| slots for text and two
\cmd|\t| sequences for literal tab characters. \cmd|\n| ends
the record with a newline. When \cmd|printf| has more arguments
than slots, it reuses the format. Here every three arguments
form one row. The empty quoted argument supplies an entry
with no characters, so there are still three fields in the
ladder row. The quote marks are for the shell, not part of
the resulting file. A compound item name is still one field
even though it contains a space.

\begin{terminal}
cat loans.tsv
\end{terminal}

Take the apparent alignment as a hint at best. A tab moves
the cursor to the next tab stop, so it doesn't always look
like the same number of spaces. To make each boundary visible,
try this diagnostic view:

\begin{terminal}
sed -n 'l' loans.tsv
\end{terminal}

The lowercase letter \texttt{l} is the list command of \cmd|sed|;
\cmd|-n| suppresses its usual printing so you see only the
diagnostic view. GNU sed displays tabs as \cmd|\t| and ends
each displayed line with a dollar sign; other implementations
can differ in how they represent nonprinting characters.
A tab is one character regardless of its visual width. In the
ladder row, find two consecutive tabs, with no shelf text
between them (Figure~\ref{fig:21-boundaries}). That empty field must remain in its proper slot
or the state will end up in the wrong column.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-21/boundaries.tikz}
\caption{A TSV field can contain spaces or be empty; the tabs fix its position.}
\label{fig:21-boundaries}
\VisualDescription{The ladder row contains the item Step ladder, a tab, an empty shelf field, another tab and the state out.}
\end{figure}

\section{Select fields without changing row order}
\label{sec:21-cut}
\cmd|cut -f| chooses numbered fields. In field mode it assumes
tabs unless you specify another delimiter. The list of field
numbers is one argument; commas separate individual positions.
Count from 1, not 0.

\begin{terminal}
cut -f 1,3 loans.tsv
\end{terminal}
\begin{terminaloutput}
item        state
Hand saw    out
Clamp set   in
Step ladder out
Out bin     in
\end{terminaloutput}

This printed output shows the values with illustrative spacing;
the actual field separator on standard output is still a tab.
The report contains the header and four data rows, in the
original order. A field list is not an instruction to reorder
columns: \cmd|cut -f 3,1| still prints selected columns in the
order they appeared in the input. It also can't select one field
conditionally based on the value of another. If a recipe says
``give me the item \emph{if} the state is out,'' there is a row
selection question as well as a column selection question.

A delimiter is a character that separates fields. It is not
a group of neighboring spaces, and \cmd|cut| does not recognize
quoted CSV fields. To see what a suitable non-tab delimiter
looks like, try a small example containing a colon between
the label and the value:

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'north:open' 'east:closed' | cut -d ':' -f 2
\end{terminal}

The results should be \texttt{open} and \texttt{closed}.
\cmd|-d ':'| declares the delimiter. This is useful only if
colons have that meaning consistently. In a row formatted
as \texttt{north:open:weekends}, what does the second colon
mean? A second delimiter would make a third field; it could
not remain part of field 2 unless a different format or parser
defined rules for it.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'north:open' 'incomplete row' | cut -d ':' -f 2
printf '%s\n' 'north:open' 'incomplete row' | cut -s -d ':' -f 2
\end{terminal}

By default, cut leaves lines without the specified delimiter
unchanged. That can be a surprise: a line that has no second
field appears in a second-field report. Add \cmd|-s| to
suppress lines with no delimiter. It is not general validation:
a row that has one tab may still be one field short. If your
goal is an audit, do not hide bad rows unless you record their
number or report them separately.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.22in}Y}
\toprule
Question & Useful operation \\
\midrule
Only fields 1 and 3? & \texttt{cut -f 1,3} for TSV, with original row and field order. \\
Only rows whose state is out? & Compare the state field in awk, rather than searching the line for the word. \\
Fields in a new order? & Use awk to print the fields in the requested order. \\
Two synchronized columns in separate files? & Use paste after verifying that corresponding rows already line up. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Select rows by a value}
\label{sec:21-awk}
\cmd|grep| from Chapter~\ref{ch:19} is useful if you remember a
word somewhere in a line. Here you already know where the
word belongs: in the state field. A case-insensitive search
for \texttt{out} would find the item \texttt{Out bin}, even
though it is marked \texttt{in}. A tab-aware comparison can
distinguish the two.

\begin{terminal}
awk -F '\t' '$3 == "out" { print $1 }' loans.tsv
\end{terminal}
\begin{terminaloutput}
Hand saw
Step ladder
\end{terminaloutput}

This is a complete little \cmd|awk| program supplied as a
quoted argument. \cmd|-F '\t'| gives awk a field separator
represented by a tab escape. The single quotes around the
program prevent the shell from expanding \cmd|$3| or \cmd|$1|.
Here those notations mean the third and first field of the
current row. The double quotes around \cmd|"out"| are part
of the awk program; they say the value to compare against is
the string \texttt{out}. The two equal signs compare the
values. A single equal sign assigns a value and would not
ask the same question. Curly braces group the action:
when the comparison is true, print the first field.

Read the program \emph{from left to right} as a rule: if
the state on this row is out, print the item's name. Awk
then applies the rule to each row, one after another. It
neither needs a loop typed on the command line nor evaluates
the words it reads from the file as shell commands
(Figure~\ref{fig:21-rule}).
The \texttt{state} header does not compare equal to
\texttt{out}, so this command happens to skip it.
That is acceptable here but implicit; for a report that
must include the header once, give the header its own rule.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-21/rule.tikz}
\caption{Filter on the state field, then print only the item field.}
\label{fig:21-rule}
\VisualDescription{Both example rows enter the rule. The state out passes and prints Hand saw; state in fails and prints nothing.}
\end{figure}

\begin{terminal}
awk -F '\t' -v OFS='\t' \
  'NR == 1 || $3 == "out" { print $3, $1 }' loans.tsv
\end{terminal}

This version prints the header and matching rows, with
state first and item second. \cmd|NR| counts input records,
so \cmd|NR == 1| is true on the first line. The two vertical
bars mean ``or'' inside the quoted program: either the
header or an open loan qualifies. The \cmd|-v| option sets
the named awk variable before reading the input. \cmd|OFS|
is the output field separator. A comma between expressions
in this \cmd|print| inserts that separator, specified here
as a tab; setting \cmd|-F| alone would not change the
default output separator of one space.

For a diagnostic report, ask for rows that do not have
three fields. \cmd|NF| is the number of fields; \cmd|!=|
means ``not equal.'' \cmd|$0| stands for the whole current
record, not field number zero. Quoting the awk rule still
matters even if it would happen to run correctly in a shell
with unusual expansion rules.

\begin{terminal}
awk -F '\t' 'NF != 3 { print NR, $0 }' loans.tsv
printf 'status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

The fixture produces no lines and the status should be
zero. Unlike \cmd|grep|, awk does not use exit status 1
to say that no record matched a condition. This particular
validation check looks for field count only. It does not
detect misspelled states, duplicate items, or absent rows;
and a row with an empty field can still pass it. Each
additional validity rule needs to come from what the
sheet is supposed to mean.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.28in}Y}
\toprule
Awk notation & Meaning in these examples \\
\midrule
\texttt{\$1}, \texttt{\$3} & First and third fields of the current record. \\
\texttt{\$0} & The entire current record. \\
\texttt{NR} & How many records have been read, including this one. \\
\texttt{NF} & How many fields the current record has. \\
\texttt{OFS} & Separator used by a print action with comma-separated expressions. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Combine columns only when rows correspond}
The \cmd|paste| utility writes adjacent columns from input
files in parallel. By default it inserts a tab. Do not use
this to merge two spreadsheets merely because they have the
same number of lines. In this example the files are made by
reading the same source twice without a change in row order.

\begin{terminal}
cut -f 1 loans.tsv > items.txt
cut -f 3 loans.tsv > states.txt
paste states.txt items.txt
\end{terminal}

Each row begins with its state and ends with its item; unlike
the filtered report, this view includes both open and returned
loans. The header reads \texttt{state} then \texttt{item}.
The names following \cmd|paste| determine the column order.
If an input is shorter, empty fields can appear for its
missing lines; the tool does not report a missing key.
It also cannot tell whether \texttt{states.txt} came from
another day's export (Figure~\ref{fig:21-paste}).

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-21/paste.tikz}
\caption{Paste pairs by line position, not by the identity of an item.}
\label{fig:21-paste}
\VisualDescription{The second line in each input is paired with the other second line; the command does not check the reason those lines correspond.}
\end{figure}

\begin{dialoguebox}{Which row goes with which?}
\Brian{I sorted the names, then pasted the states beside them. The columns line up.}
\Stephen{They do visually. But what told paste that a state belonged to a name?}
\Brian{The line number. Doesn't sorting use line numbers too?}
\Stephen{Sorting moves records to new positions. The states in the other file didn't move with them.}
\Brian{Then my report can be neat and entirely wrong.}
\Stephen{Yes. Pair rows first and sort whole paired records, or match on a verified identifier.}
\Brian{I'll keep each item with its state until I can verify the pair.}
\DialogueConclusion{A shared row number is not an identifier.}
\end{dialoguebox}

To adapt the technique to a real export, inspect a small set
of first and last lines and ask for the format description.
Tabs, spaces and commas are not interchangeable. If you
actually have CSV rather than TSV, a comma inside a quoted
name is not a field boundary. For a CSV-specific reader, see
Chapter~\ref{ch:24} after this practice. Treat empty
shelves as missing data, not a reason to fill in a value
you didn't observe. Preserve the original and label any report
derived from a filtered or repaired copy.

\section{Check the shape before counting the results}
The first open-loans report was short enough to verify by
reading both files. A bigger sheet needs a check of its
structure before you interpret the extracted columns. Start
at the header. Three fields with the wrong names or order
will pass a field-count check. The third field could now be
a shelf rather than a state, in which case the original
filter quietly leaves out every open loan. A header check
should identify that as the wrong export, not as good news
about overdue tools.

For the fixture, all the expected labels are known.
\cmd|head -n 1| prints the first physical line without
scanning the rest. Use the diagnostic display you tried
earlier to confirm the tab boundaries. Then print the
positions of any data records with a state other than the
two supported values. Use both checks rather than trying
to infer structure from the final report alone.

\begin{terminal}
head -n 1 loans.tsv | sed -n 'l'
awk -F '\t' \
  'NR > 1 && $3 != "in" && $3 != "out" { print NR }' loans.tsv
\end{terminal}

The first line should identify the header names item,
shelf and state in that order. The second command should be
silent. In the awk rule, \cmd|>| compares numbers and
\cmd|&&| means ``and'': this is a data row, it is not in,
and it is not out. That last pair of conditions catches
spellings like \texttt{OUT} and \texttt{out } with an
extra space. It also catches rows without field 3. It
does not replace the separate field-count check, which
can catch an extra tab after a perfectly good state. A
diagnostic that prints line numbers instead of all field
contents can be easier to share without revealing
individual entries in a larger report.

When you find a suspicious line number, go back to the
source with its context. Do not start by editing the
source to make it fit the command. An empty item name may
need a volunteer's follow-up, while an empty shelf for a
step ladder might mean it is hung on a wall instead of a
shelf. Ask whether the missing value is allowed. A program
that fills both with a default could make two quite
different problems disappear from view.

\subsection{Pick a presentation without changing the data}
The earlier report used a tab so someone could feed it
back into another tab-aware utility. A report for people
at the checkout desk can be simpler. Choose a readable
sentence for each item, and keep the state in the
selection rather than in the displayed name. In this
awk example a quoted string contains fixed text, and the
comma in the print action joins that text to the first
field with the usual single blank.

\begin{terminal}
awk -F '\t' '$3 == "out" { print "Still out:", $1 }' loans.tsv
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{Still out: Hand saw} and then
\texttt{Still out: Step ladder}. This output is useful at
a glance, but it is not TSV. Don't rely on another tool
splitting it into columns without agreement on a new
format. For instance, the space after ``Hand'' belongs
to the item's name, and a colon could belong to the name
in another sheet. A portable plain-text view for people
and an unambiguous export for machines are different
deliverables, even when they come from the same rule.

If you have to save either deliverable, redirect to a
new name, verify the command's status, and count or inspect
the rows as a separate step. For a machine-readable report
with a header, there should be three rows in this fixture:
one header and two records. More generally, compare
the number of reported records with the number that meet
the rule, not with the total number of records in the
sheet. If the counts disagree, check whether you projected
before filtering, sorted only one column, or mixed up
which export you treated as the source. That sequence of
checks often reveals more than a quick glance at the
first plausible item.

\subsection{Keep related fields together during sorting}
The checkout desk may ask for names in order. Start
with whole records, then reorder those records by item.
Don't sort just the item list and try to recover the
states by their old positions. For other lists, it may
be easier to sort a projected report that retains a
stable identifier. The important question is which
fields must stay associated through every transformation.
A checkout sheet with two copies of the same saw needs
something to distinguish the two loans; the name alone
does not identify a particular copy.

Try sorting the fixture after dropping its header and
keeping the state with the name. The first awk pattern
is true after the header and the action prints the whole
record. A \cmd|print| with nothing after it is shorthand
for \cmd|print $0|. The second stage sorts the complete
lines; the first field is the item, so it determines the
order for these distinct names. Finally, cut selects the
item and state columns in that order.

\begin{terminal}
awk 'NR > 1 { print }' loans.tsv |
  LC_ALL=C sort | cut -f 1,3
\end{terminal}

Expect Clamp set, Hand saw, Out bin and Step ladder, with
the correct state shown beside each. The original order
in the file does not change. If you need a header in a
saved version, handle it separately; sorting a header
along with records makes its position an accident of
spelling. Before handing off a saved report, check that
one known record from the source still has the right
state in the new order.

\begin{furtherreading}
\ReadingItem{POSIX cut}{Field selections, delimiters and the treatment of lines without delimiters}{https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/utilities/cut.html}
\item \textbf{POSIX paste}. See the parallel-output rules and handling of unequal input lengths:
  \WebLink{https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/utilities/paste.html}{paste specification}.
\ReadingItem{POSIX awk}{Records, field splitting, built-in variables and actions}{https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/utilities/awk.html}
\end{furtherreading}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Know the separator before selecting fields.
\item Use field-based conditions when the position of a value matters.
\item Paste aligned rows only; sort whole records to preserve their relationships.
\end{takeaways}

\begin{exercise}{Find shelves, not states}
\label{ex:21-shelves}
Print the item and shelf columns. Expect the ladder to have an
empty shelf without disappearing from the report. Nothing is
written to disk.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
cut -f 1,2 loans.tsv | sed -n 'l'
\end{terminal}
The ladder row should have a tab after its name and no shelf text.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Change the qualifying state}
\label{ex:21-returned}
Print the names of items marked \texttt{in}. Expect two names,
including one that starts with the word Out. No cleanup is needed.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
awk -F '\t' '$3 == "in" { print $1 }' loans.tsv
\end{terminal}
The results are Clamp set and Out bin.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Catch a damaged row}
\label{ex:21-damaged}
Send a one-line test record with just two fields to the field
count rule. Expect line number 1 followed by the damaged row.
Use a pipeline; leave the original file unchanged.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\t%s\n' 'Test item' A1 |
  awk -F '\t' 'NF != 3 { print NR, $0 }'
\end{terminal}
This test creates no extra files.
\end{solution}

\paragraph{Cleanup.}
Verify with \cmd|pwd| that this is the disposable \texttt{utp21}
directory named in \cmd|"$practice"|. Remove only the three
files created here and decline unrecognized names. If you skipped
an example, a missing-file diagnostic is expected.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i ./loans.tsv ./items.txt ./states.txt
cd /tmp
rmdir "$practice"
\end{terminal}

If the directory is not empty, inspect the leftovers. Type
\cmd|exit| only if you started a separate practice shell.
